//RS /// The receiver type the built-in table dispatches on.
//RS ///
//RS /// `tir_recv_jet_ty` answers `None` for a struct field read, so this helper
//RS /// recovers the declaring field type before dispatch. A recovered type is
//RS /// accepted only when the canonical collection table has the same method row;
//RS /// an unrecoverable or mismatched type returns `None` rather than selecting a
//RS /// guessed List operation. This closes the old String-field/List-surface hole
//RS /// at the dispatch boundary, once for every shared method name.
//RS ///
//RS /// Sema already resolved this receiver — it type-checked the call against
//RS /// `Collections::builtin_method_arg_types(String, "replace")` — it just does not
//RS /// persist the type: a builtin's `recv_type` stays `None`. So the field type is
//RS /// re-read from the DECLARING tables (`struct_field_type`: user structs, then
//RS /// every core record), and is trusted ONLY when the same builtin table sema used
//RS /// has a row for `(that type, method, arity)`. If the exact field type cannot be
//RS /// recovered, this resolver returns `None`; it never selects the legacy List arm.
//RS ///
//RS /// Deliberately NOT folded into `tir_recv_jet_ty`: its other callers read that
//RS /// partiality as a fact (the for-in `lines` split recognizes `child.stdout` by
//RS /// its BASE, the mutable-place hint, the view-owner peeks), and this is the one
//RS /// place a receiver type becomes an op.
